<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Machilipatnam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Masulipatnam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Machilipatnam, historically Masulipatnam, is a port town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> delta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Under the Golconda Sultanate it was the main outlet for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel</a> textiles, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> (1605), English (1611) and French set up factories there. The British took it from the French in 1759, and the town is known for its kalamkari printed cloth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/machilipatnam.jpg" alt="Machilipatnam" width="320" height="151" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Satyach73 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robertson_Square.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Machilipatnam lies on the south-eastern, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel</a>, coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> at the mouth of the River Krishna on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, about 60 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayawada">Vijayawada</a>. In the 17th century it was known as Masulipatnam, Masulipatam, Masula and Bandar, the last from the Persian word for port. In ancient times it was also called Maesolia. It is the administrative headquarters of Krishna district.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town has existed since at least the 3rd century BCE, the Satavahana period, when according to Ptolemy it was known as Maisolos. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a> calls it Masalia. Sea trade flourished at the port, and the word muslin is derived from the name Maisolos. Muslin was a significant source of income and a favourite of Roman traders, and several Roman coins have been found in excavations of Buddhist towns near the town.</p><p>Machilipatnam served as the primary sea port of the Kingdom of Golkonda, an outlet for textiles produced in the Godavari Delta. It was a major source of income for the kingdom and contributed to its prosperity in the 1620s and 1630s. From the 16th century European traders settled there, and in the 17th century it was a major trading port for the Portuguese, British, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and French. It was known for minting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins and for exporting diamonds and textiles. By the end of the 17th century the port had fallen into decline, which coincided with the Mughal conquest of Golkonda, though mismanagement before the conquest may also have been a factor.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salabat-jung">Salabat Jung</a>, the son of Nizam-ul-Mulk Asaf Jah I, owed his throne to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-east-india-company">French East India Company</a> and granted it in return the district of Kondavid, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntur">Guntur</a>, and soon afterwards the remainder of the Circars. The British captured the Circars from the French in 1759 and returned them to the Nizam, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> kept Masulipatnam, which it had taken in the Siege of Masulipatam. The town became the headquarters of Masulipatnam district, from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> district was formed in 1859 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> state. On 1 November 1864 a giant wave damaged the port, and the cyclone of that year is said to have killed at least 30,000 people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Machilipatnam is known for its handloom industry and for Machilipatnam Kalamkari, hand-dyed block-painting on cloth, which is carried out at the nearby town of Pedana and has a geographical indication. Boat building and fishing are other local trades. The town has the ruined buildings built by the Europeans who settled there, and it is home to Shia Muslim imambargahs, some more than 600 years old, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashura">Ashura</a> is observed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Machilipatnam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It set up a factory at <b>Masulipatnam</b> in 1611, gained rights at Surat from the Mughal Emperor Jahangir in 1612, built a factory at Madras in 1640 and leased Bombay island in 1668; after the Anglo-Mughal War Aurangzeb let it settle at Calcutta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dutch East India Company (VOC) opened a factory at <b>Masulipatnam</b> in 1605 and in 1610 obtained permission from the Vijayanagara ruler to settle at Pulicat, where Fort Geldria was built in 1613.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-coromandel">Dutch Coromandel</a></li><li><blockquote>Golconda was famous for its diamond mines, which produced the Koh-i-Noor, and for cotton textiles exported from <b>Masulipatnam</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/golconda-sultanate">Golconda Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
